            <ArticleTitle>Renal hypertension: an unusual cause for a common problem.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>711-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00431-013-1957-z</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 9-year-old girl with longstanding headaches presented acutely with rash, which disappeared quickly on treatment with oral phenoxymethylpenicillin. It was attributed to streptococcal infection as group A streptococcus was isolated from throat swab. She was incidentally found to have high blood pressure on routine screening on admission. Subsequently, 'fibromuscular dysplasia' was confirmed on renal angiogram, which showed a characteristic beaded appearance. It is a good clinical practice to check blood pressure in any child seen for whatever reason.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
